finish('seese-mso-one-decidability-clique-width',
 id='TCS-6654',title='Seese’s conjecture for MSO₁ and clique-width',area='Structural graph theory',criterion='characterization',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='For every isomorphism-closed class C of finite simple undirected graphs, does decidability of its MSO₁ satisfiability problem imply that some constant k bounds the clique-width of every graph in C?',
 definitions='An MSO₁ sentence is built from vertex equality, adjacency E(x,y), membership x∈X in vertex sets, Boolean connectives, and quantification over individual vertices and subsets of the vertex set. There is no built-in vertex order, edge-set quantification, parity predicate or other counting extension. For a fixed class C, Sat_MSO₁(C) consists of all finite encoded MSO₁ sentences φ for which some G∈C satisfies φ. Decidable means that one Turing machine, allowed to depend on C, halts and answers correctly for every input sentence. C itself is not an input to that machine; no complexity bound is required. By negating sentences this is equivalent to deciding the theory Th_MSO₁(C)={φ: every G∈C satisfies φ}. A k-expression builds a labeled graph from single vertices with labels in [k], disjoint union, relabeling every vertex of one label to another, and adding all edges between two distinct labels. The clique-width cw(G) is the smallest k permitting such an expression after labels are forgotten; set cw(∅)=0. The conclusion is ∃k∀G∈C cw(G)≤k, with k depending on C. No hereditary, minor-closed, bounded-degree or finite-forbidden-subgraph assumption is imposed.',
 answer_criterion='Prove the implication for every such graph class, or give one class of unbounded clique-width together with a correct terminating algorithm for its entire MSO₁ satisfiability problem. A single finite graph cannot refute a uniform width bound for a class. Model checking on a supplied graph, or decidability with a promise about sentence size, is not the premise. Results requiring parity predicates or restricted graph classes do not prove the full statement.',
 context=[
 paragraph('An MSO₁ sentence can describe a graph property by quantifying over sets of vertices. Three-colorability, for example, can be expressed by choosing three sets that partition the vertices and requiring each set to be independent. Checking such a sentence on one finite graph is always decidable by exhaustive search. The premise here is much stronger: from a sentence alone, decide whether it holds in at least one member of an entire, potentially infinite graph class.'),
 paragraph('Clique-width measures how many vertex labels are needed to construct a graph using a small repertoire of operations. Labels identify groups of vertices that receive the same future edge additions. Dense graphs can have small clique-width: a complete graph can be built with two labels by inserting one vertex, joining it to all previous vertices, and merging its label. Thus the conjectured structural restriction is not a sparsity condition.'),
 paragraph('The classical treewidth theorem provides the motivation. Seese proves structural restrictions from decidability of monadic theories, and the version allowing quantification over edge sets forces bounded treewidth. Grid-minor machinery supports that argument. The MSO₁ conjecture replaces edge-set quantification by vertex-set quantification and asks for bounded clique-width instead. Transferring the proof requires a logical way of recovering the appropriate obstruction.','seese'),
 paragraph('Bounded-width descriptions support decidability because graph properties can be translated into properties of construction trees. In particular, for each fixed k the full class of graphs of clique-width at most k has decidable MSO₁ satisfiability. But the conjecture is only a necessary-condition statement for an arbitrary class C. One cannot infer that every subclass of a bounded-width class has decidable theory.','parity'),
 paragraph('For a simple reason, such a converse would be false. Choose an undecidable set S of positive integers, and let C contain exactly the edgeless graphs whose numbers of vertices belong to S. All those graphs have clique-width one. A sentence specifying exactly n vertices would decide whether n∈S if satisfiability over C were decidable. Restricting which sizes occur can encode undecidability even when each individual graph has trivial structure.'),
 paragraph('Courcelle–Oum prove the implication after extending MSO₁ with the predicate Even(X), saying that a vertex set has even cardinality. Their vertex-minor and isotropic-system machinery establishes bounded clique-width from decidability of this stronger logic. This strengthens the premise: a decision procedure for ordinary MSO₁ sentences need not handle parity sentences. Therefore their theorem supplies substantial progress while leaving the exact statement here open.','parity'),
 paragraph('Hereditary classes, which are closed under taking induced subgraphs, are a useful focus even though the card quantifies over all classes. If satisfiability is decidable on C, it is also decidable on its hereditary closure: ask whether some vertex subset induces a graph satisfying the input sentence, using a relativized MSO₁ formula. Unbounded clique-width is preserved on passing to that larger class. Thus a proof covering all hereditary classes would suffice.'),
 paragraph('Dawar–Sankaran develop a stronger route through interpreting arbitrarily large grids. Their Theorem 3.2 reduces that stronger assertion to two settings: antichains of unbounded clique-width under induced-subgraph containment, and minimal hereditary classes of unbounded clique-width. They verify the grid interpretation for the known minimal families studied in their paper. This is a research program and collection of cases, not a completed classification of all such families.','hereditary'),
 paragraph('The inherited ICALP 2025 paper by Mählmann settles a different boundary: on hereditary classes, bounded shrub-depth characterizes coincidence of first-order and monadic second-order expressive power. Its conclusion formulates a stronger conjectural relationship between clique-width and MSO dependence, explaining how parts of that conjecture would imply Seese’s. Shrub-depth and clique-width are distinct parameters, and a theorem about expressive power does not by itself decide satisfiability.','primary'),
 paragraph('Duron–Mählmann–Toruńczyk’s July 2026 preprint proves that every hereditary 2-WQO class has bounded clique-width. Here 2-WQO means that every infinite sequence of graphs with two possible vertex labels contains an earlier graph embedding as a label-preserving induced subgraph of a later one. This resolves a related structural conjecture, but it starts from a well-quasi-ordering hypothesis. It does not turn an arbitrary MSO₁ decision procedure into such an ordering property.','wqo'),
 paragraph('The unresolved implication asks whether decidable graph theories always have bounded constructional complexity. A proof would show that broad logical decidability cannot arise from some entirely different mechanism on classes of unbounded clique-width. A counterexample would reveal precisely such a mechanism. Either outcome would change how structural graph theory explains the limits of automated reasoning over whole families of finite structures.')],
 why='A major proposed structural necessity for logical decidability, connecting graph decompositions, finite model theory and algorithms over infinite families of finite inputs.',
 importance=dict(score=95,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A foundational conjecture asking whether bounded clique-width is unavoidable whenever an entire graph class has decidable vertex-set monadic logic.'),
 progress=[step('1991','Seese establishes foundational connections between decidable monadic graph theories and bounded structural width, and formulates the conjectural MSO₁ boundary.','seese'),step('2007','Courcelle–Oum prove that decidable MSO₁ with parity predicates implies bounded clique-width.','parity'),step('2020–2025','Dawar–Sankaran develop the antichain/minimal-hereditary-class reduction for the stronger grid-interpretation conjecture; preprint 2020, CSL 2022, journal volume 2025.','hereditary'),step('2025','Mählmann characterizes bounded shrub-depth through logical expressiveness and records a stronger MSO-dependence conjecture that would imply Seese’s.','primary'),step('12–26 July 2026','Duron–Mählmann–Toruńczyk prove bounded clique-width for hereditary 2-WQO classes; the hypothesis differs from decidability of MSO₁ satisfiability.','wqo')],
 references=[ref('primary','Forbidden Induced Subgraphs for Bounded Shrub-Depth and the Expressive Power of MSO','Nikolas Mählmann',2025,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2025.167','Inherited source; ICALP 2025, 167:1–167:18; concluding MSO-Dependence discussion and Conjecture 40'),ref('seese','The structure of the models of decidable monadic theories of graphs','Detlef Seese',1991,'https://doi.org/10.1016/0168-0072(91)90054-P','Annals of Pure and Applied Logic 53(2), 169–195; see also the introductory account in Courcelle–Oum for the MSO₂ theorem and MSO₁ conjecture'),ref('parity','Vertex-minors, monadic second-order logic, and a conjecture by Seese','Bruno Courcelle; Sang-il Oum',2007,'https://www.labri.fr/perso/courcell/Textes1/BC-Oum%282007%29.pdf','Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B 97(1), 91–126; introduction and Theorem 5.6; DOI 10.1016/j.jctb.2006.04.003'),ref('hereditary','MSO undecidability for hereditary classes of unbounded clique-width','Anuj Dawar; Abhisekh Sankaran',2025,'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2023.103700','European Journal of Combinatorics 123, article 103700 (2025); 2023 online publication, 2020 preprint arXiv:2011.02894; Theorem 3.2 in the accepted manuscript'),ref('wqo','Hereditary 2-WQO Graph Classes Have Bounded Clique-Width','Julien Duron; Nikolas Mählmann; Szymon Toruńczyk',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10939','Posted 12 July 2026; v2 dated 26 July; hereditary two-label well-quasi-ordering hypothesis')],
 status='source_open',status_note='Reviewed through 11 September 2026. Seese’s MSO₁ decidability implication remains unresolved in the checked primary literature. The parity extension, the shrub-depth expressiveness characterization and the July 2026 2-WQO result have distinct hypotheses or conclusions.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
